Object.assign(window.VAULT_NOTES = window.VAULT_NOTES || {}, {"A desert claim requires both aboutness and fitness":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>For a desert claim to hold, the basis of the claim must be about the person who makes it, which is usually cashed out as responsibility, and there must be a natural fit between that basis and the thing deserved.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>These two conditions are what allow desert claims to be evaluated rather than merely asserted, and most objections to desert attack one or the other.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Aboutness fails when the basis is a circumstance rather than an exercise of agency, which is the form the Rawlsian objection takes. Fitness fails when the reward has no natural relation to the basis, as when a prize for one excellence is paid in a currency belonging to another.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Some people must deserve something\">Some people must deserve something</a><ul><li>The pre-theoretical claim these conditions are meant to regiment.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Career open to talents does not mitigate social nor natural contingencies\">Career open to talents does not mitigate social nor natural contingencies</a><ul><li>The attack on aboutness.</li></ul></li></ul>","Bad Samaritans (book)":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>This book discusses the many downsides to the neoliberal world order that has been established.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is an important book that counters the traditional, mainstream economic view of neoliberal global policy.</p><h4>Ideas &amp; Themes</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Material incentives are not the only thing that motivates people to produce new ideas\">Material incentives are not the only thing that motivates people to produce new ideas</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Due to the winner-takes-all nature of patent systems, waste is created through duplicate research\">Due to the winner-takes-all nature of patent systems, waste is created through duplicate research</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Most industries do not require patents to generate new knowledge; however, preexisting patents slow innovation\">Most industries do not require patents to generate new knowledge; however, preexisting patents slow innovation</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Inventors have an incentive to lower the bar of originality for patents and IPRs\">Inventors have an incentive to lower the bar of originality for patents and IPRs</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Interlocking patents is a barrier to innovation\">Interlocking patents is a barrier to innovation</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"International IPR and patent laws benefit incumbents\">International IPR and patent laws benefit incumbents</a></li></ul>","Belief in meritocracy makes people behave more unfairly":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A belief in meritocracy makes people act more unfairly towards one another.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This can be used to support arguments that oppose meritocracy.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Scientists have designed experiments where participants play fake games of skill.</p><ul><li>In such cases, they are more likely to distribute winnings to themselves when they believed that they had won as a result for their superior skill compared to when they thought that it was just luck.</li></ul><p><code>But doesn't the very fact that this instinct evolved mean that it is in fact the case that, more likely than not, the more meritorious person deserves more rewards? It seems like we are merely exploiting edge cases in our intuition in order to come up with these results.</code></p><h4>References</h4>","Commerce is not ‘natural aquisition’":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Aristotle’s idea that commerce is something that is bad, since it is not natural, given that commerce does not use the items that it involves according to its “true” purpose, but rather its secondary purpose, that of being able to be traded.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This laid the groundwork for Marxist economic thought in the 19th century, and was the putative belief regarding the nature of economic for the period before the Enlightenment.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Aristotle claims that this unnatural acquisition is the result of people merely pursuing life, and not living well. When people merely seek bodily satisfaction, the result is commerce.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The concept of justice is most fully realized only when applied in a city\">The concept of justice is most fully realized only when applied in a city</a><ul><li>Regards political justice.</li></ul></li></ul>","Conflict is sharpened by differences in culture":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Conflicts between nations are often made worse or more intense due to civilizational/cultural differences between the belligerents.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This may explain why cross-cultural conflicts often result in greater violence and destruction.</p><h4>Examples</h4><p>During the wars after the dissolution of Yugoslavia, there was brutal fault line conflicts between the various new nations that formed, including mass genocides of people of different cultures.</p><ul><li>The war in Yugoslavia is an example of a complex fault line war, where there is an intricate network of supporting parties on both sides of the conflict that do not actually fight.</li></ul><p> In the diagram above, $A_{1}$ and $B_{1}$ represent the actual parties engaged in the conflict, while $A_{2}$ and $B_{2}$ are secondary and $A_{3}$ and $B_{3}$ are tertiary actors.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Culture is the largest and most prominent cleavage in societies\">Culture is the largest and most prominent cleavage in societies</a><ul><li>This would explain why this note is the case.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The rejection of Western culture has lead to various ‘affirmations’ of Asian culture\">The rejection of Western culture has lead to various ‘affirmations’ of Asian culture</a></li><li>This affirmation of Asian culture could mean that when there are future conflicts between Asian cultures and Western ones, it could be more brutal due to the cultural difference.</li></ul>","Continence is the ability to for one’s reason to overpower one’s desires":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Continence, or self-control, is the ability to control one’s desires with reason, so that even though one may have base desires, such a desire is not acted upon due to the restraining influence of reason.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is one of the important moral states that Aristotle identifies.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A variety of factors could cause us to be incontinent, or lack self-control\">A variety of factors could cause us to be incontinent, or lack self-control</a><ul><li>When desire overpowers rationality, one becomes incontinent.</li></ul></li></ul>","During Prophase I, homologous chromosomes meet in a process called synapsis":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>After S phase, there are two copies of every chromatid. Since you have two copies for each chromosome (each one having come from your parents) even before that, you are left with 4 chromatids. During Prophase I, the sister chromatids come together to form a tetrad (four chromatids) in a process called synapsis.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is important, since if the homologous chromosomes do not meet together during Prophase I, they would not be separated correctly in order for Meiosis II to occur.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"When two genes are farther apart, it is more likely that recombination will occur\">When two genes are farther apart, it is more likely that recombination will occur</a><ul><li>What synapsis makes possible. Once the tetrad forms, crossing over can occur, and distance governs how often.</li></ul></li></ul>","Free trade is generally beneficial for those involved":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Free trade increases the size of the total surplus of the economy.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains a fundamental concept in economics.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>In trade, the international price that is set is lower than the domestic price, therefore increasing consumer surplus. While this also decreases producer surplus, the decrease is not commensurable with the increase. This, however, poses the fundamental problem in trade, which is how to recompense the losing party.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Private property facilitates trade\">Private property facilitates trade</a><ul><li>Trade requires private property.</li></ul></li></ul>","Hannibal was an excellent tactician, but poor grand strategist":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>While Hannibal was a more than competent military tactician, as can be seen through the various strategems he employed at battles such as the Trebia or Cannae, he was not a great grand strategist.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains the Carthaginian defeat during the Second Punic War.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Despite the many victories achieved by Hannibal during his campaign in Italy, he was enable to capitalize on the victories in a strategic sense. In the end, the war turned into a constant grind between Hannibal’s slowly dwindling forces and the Romans, as Hannibal by the latter half of the Italian campaign was unable to force decisive victories on the Romans. He was never able to use his battlefield victories to push the Romans into capitulation.</p><h4>References</h4>","Ideas are getting harder to find":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>New ideas are getting more difficult to find.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This could have massive implications for productivity growth in the future, as more and more resources are required to acquire new information.</p><h4>Graphs</h4><p> </p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Labor productivity grows faster in manufacturing than service sectors\">Labor productivity grows faster in manufacturing than service sectors</a><ul><li>Related to the idea of productivity. Mostly likely since it is easier for technological advancements to benefit manufacturing than it is to benefit service sectors.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A drop in productivity growth is hugely detrimental to overall economic growth\">A drop in productivity growth is hugely detrimental to overall economic growth</a><ul><li>Illustrates the impact that the events described herein could have.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Economic growth is not solely caused by productivity growth\">Economic growth is not solely caused by productivity growth</a><ul><li>At the same time, it should be recognized that economic growth is not complete related to productivity growth.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Baumol&#x27;s Cost Disease slows economic growth due to increasing input costs in low productivity industries\">Baumol's Cost Disease slows economic growth due to increasing input costs in low productivity industries</a><ul><li>A related idea that describes how low productivity could have an effect on the economy.</li></ul></li></ul>","Imperial Chinese astronomy was predictive, rather than causal-theoretic":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A main point of difference between Imperial Chinese astronomy and the astronomy that was occurring in the West was that while Chinese astronomy was largely predictive, i.e., people studied astronomy in order to <em>predict</em> the arrange of celestial elements, the Wests’ study was more <em>casual-theoretic</em>, i.e., investigations into the reasons for particular motions.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This may explain the lack of a scientific revolution in China.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>While Chinese astronomy was focused on predicting future events, European astronomy was much more focused on understanding the reasons for why things worked. This was largely because of cultural differences and the fact that only the predictive aspect was of interest to the Chinese state (since it formed part of the Mandate of Heaven of the ruler to be able to predict astronomical events), and it was banned for private individuals.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Imperial Chinese state’s monopoly on knowledge prevented a scientific revolution\">The Imperial Chinese state’s monopoly on knowledge prevented a scientific revolution</a><ul><li>Chinese astronomy was monopolized by the state.</li></ul></li></ul>","Oedipus the King (play)":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Follows Oedipus, the confident ruler of Thebes, as he tries to save his city from a devastating plague by finding the murderer of the former king, Laius. As he investigates, he uncovers a terrible truth: he himself unknowingly killed Laius and married his own mother, Jocasta, fulfilling the prophecy he had spent his life trying to avoid. The play is a tragic exploration of fate, truth, and the limits of human knowledge, ending with Jocasta’s suicide and Oedipus blinding himself and accepting exile.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>A major work of literature that has shaped philosophy, future literature, and theses in psychology.</p><h4>Ideas &amp; Themes</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"It is not always good to know the truth\">It is not always good to know the truth</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The &#x27;tragedy’ in tragic works of art often spring from the main character’s own actions\">The 'tragedy’ in tragic works of art often spring from the main character’s own actions</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Our fate is determined from birth\">Our fate is determined from birth</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A tragic protagonist can embody the political character of the society that produced them\">A tragic protagonist can embody the political character of the society that produced them</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The blind are able to see what the sighted are not\">The blind are able to see what the sighted are not</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Humanity is insignificant\">Humanity is insignificant</a></li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The pursuit of complete certainty delays action past the point where it can succeed\">The pursuit of complete certainty delays action past the point where it can succeed</a><ul><li>In this respect, Hamlet is very much like Oedipus, in that both characters seek the truth in the world. Yet, it is this truth that eventually drives them mad and leads to their downfall.</li><li>Yet, there is a crucial difference between the character of Hamlet and Oedipus: while the former is driven by his search for truth, he never acts on the result. On the other hand, Oedipus acts with no hesitation when he learns the truth.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The &#x27;tragedy’ in tragic works of art often spring from the main character’s own actions\">The 'tragedy’ in tragic works of art often spring from the main character’s own actions</a><ul><li>This is exactly the case in <em>Oedipus Rex</em>.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Tragedy can be more optimistic than comedy\">Tragedy can be more optimistic than comedy</a><ul><li>There is some semblance of optimism at the end of this play, especially in the way that Oedipus accepts his own exile.</li></ul></li></ul>","People are far more easily swayed by pathos than logos":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>People are far more able to be inspired by argumentation that appeals to their inner sense of pathos than to strict, logical argumentation.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This can profound implications for politics, since this means that people can be easily swayed by demagogues.</p><h4><em>All The King’s Men</em></h4><p>This is something that is specifically noted for Willie Stark, since when he began his speeches, much of it was simply tax figures and numbers that seemed entirely uninspiring to people. However, as his career progressed, he learned to harvest the spirit of the people through fiery polemics and generally invoking pathos far more than logos. </p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"People value present goods more than future goods\">People value present goods more than future goods</a><ul><li>This is something that demagogues often exploit.</li></ul></li></ul>","Pleasure and freedom from pain are the only desirable things; therefore, they represent the purpose of life":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The only desirable things in the world are pleasure and freedom from pain.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the core precept of utilitarianism.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>This is the fundamental principle that all utilitarian theories fundamentally subscribe to. Something is only desirable if it gives one pleasure, saves one from pain, or is a means to achieving those two. Some critics claim that happiness is unattainable. However, it must be said that:</p><ul><li>at least unhappiness is bad, and </li><li>happiness is achievable to some extent, and therefore, the claim that happiness is unattainable is at least exaggerated.</li></ul><p>Also, according to Mill, we do desire things like virtue, though this only means that happiness has something like virtue as a constituent.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Persons who are born with low but non-negative utility cannot be said to have been ‘harmed’ in their birth\">Persons who are born with low but non-negative utility cannot be said to have been ‘harmed’ in their birth</a><ul><li>This point seems to strengthen the un-paradoxical-ness of the non-identity problem.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Pleasure is not solely the Good\">Pleasure is not solely the Good</a><ul><li>In this way, Mill would disagree with Aristotle.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Pleasure perfects activities, and thus, life\">Pleasure perfects activities, and thus, life</a><ul><li>A related claim about pleasure.</li></ul></li><li><span class=\"wl-off\">Aristotle on Pleasure</span><ul><li>Aristotle’s views on pleasure.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The end goal of human life is happiness, which requires sustained virtuous action and comfortable material conditions\">The end goal of human life is happiness, which requires sustained virtuous action and comfortable material conditions</a><ul><li>Aristotle has a different conception of the end goal of life, though it is in some ways similar.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"What is good for a person is a rational long-term plan of life given reasonably favorable circumstances\">What is good for a person is a rational long-term plan of life given reasonably favorable circumstances</a><ul><li>It seems that Rawls’ view regarding the good life is pretty radically different.</li></ul></li></ul>","The 'iron triangle' of government can lead to regulatory capture":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The “iron triangle” of government, consisting of congress, the bureaucracy, and the lobbyists, may often act to benefit each other (as the three exist in a symbiotic relationship) rather than for the good of the public.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>The “iron triangle” is, as apparent in its name, difficult to break. This makes it a tough topic to address, to enact reforms to ensure that the government actually acts in the interest of the public.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><em>3 ways Trump can deliver an economic ‘golden age’ for America</em></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"20th-century American presidents have expanded presidential powers beyond their original constitutional intentions\">20th-century American presidents have expanded presidential powers beyond their original constitutional intentions</a><ul><li>If presidents become more powerful, it means that it could potentially strengthen the iron triangle.</li></ul></li></ul>","The Japanese healthcare system is highly socialized":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>In Japan, the healthcare system is highly socialized, as people are forced to purchase healthcare by the government. Additionally, the government set all prices for medical procedures. At the same time, around 50% of Japanese hospitals were in debt.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Japan’s healthcare system was radically different from many different countries, as they managed to merge a public and private system. At the same time, many of their hospitals were hugely in debt.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The fact that Japan was not subjugated by the Mongols, while China was, led to their rejection of outside influence\">The fact that Japan was not subjugated by the Mongols, while China was, led to their rejection of outside influence</a></li></ul>","The US should not blindly abide by its foreign policy commitments made during the Cold War":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The US should reevaluate the foreign policy engagement that it made during the Cold War era to see if they are still valuable today, now that the borders of conflict have shifted to civilizational ones, and not ideological ones.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This note is calling for a re-evaluation of US foreign policy.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Many of the foreign policy commitments made by the US during the Cold War were shaped by the ideological conflict during the Cold War, and thus do not reflect the new world order that is civilization-based. Examples of such involvements include:</p><ul><li>American alliance with Japan.</li><li>American alliance with Pakistan.</li><li>The West's alignment with Turkey.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Culture is the largest and most prominent cleavage in societies\">Culture is the largest and most prominent cleavage in societies</a><ul><li>This is why the US cannot continue with his ideology-driven commitments made during the Cold War.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The end of the Cold War marked a major change in global security\">The end of the Cold War marked a major change in global security</a><ul><li>With these military changes, there should be commensurate foreign policy changes.</li></ul></li></ul>","The lack of support for pro-supply solutions to the housing crisis is due to people's misunderstanding of what causes the issue":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>While people have good economic intuitions about the nature of market forces in many areas of life, one areas where this intuition seems to be missing is when it comes to housing. This leads to people blaming the wrong demographic for high housing prices.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This shows how misconceptions about the way that the economy works can have tangible and notable effects on the actual function of the economy.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>While many people think that developers and landlords are the problem behind high housing prices/high rent, the root of the problem is ultimately that lack of housing restricts supply, and ultimately leads to higher prices.</p><h4>References</h4><p>-</p>","The more electronegative an atom, the more negatively charged it is":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The more that an atom is electronegative, the more negatively charged the atom is. This maybe a partial charge ($\\delta^-$) or a full negative charge.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is hugely relevant for AP Bio.</p><h4>References</h4>","The rejection of Western culture has lead to various ‘affirmations’ of Asian culture":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The rejection of Western culture in East Asia has led to various beliefs that have been integrated into the way that Asians think about Asian culture.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains the rise in tensions across cultural boundaries.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>The US's power has declined in Asia due to a combination of the following factors:</p><ul><li>The US does not have the will to pressure Asian societies.</li><li>The US thinks concessions will be reciprocal, but Asians just see them as victories.</li><li>Asian countries have banded together in order to oppose the dominance of the US in the region.</li></ul><p>There are four such “affirmations”:</p><ol><li>Asia will have sustained economic growth that will overtake the West.</li><li>Asian culture leads to economic growth, and is thus superior to the West (specifically, the influence of Confucianism is prominent).</li><li>Relative homogeneity of Asian culture, which is again a result of influence from Confucianism.</li><li>Asian culture should be adopted by the West.</li></ol><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The West is in decline\">The West is in decline</a><ul><li>With the decline of the West comes the rise of Asia.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Many East Asian countries are dirigisme and neo-mercantilist\">Many East Asian countries are dirigisme and neo-mercantilist</a><ul><li>This is perhaps part of the culture of Asian, the thing that lead to their economic growth.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Human capital explains the success of East Asian nations\">Human capital explains the success of East Asian nations</a><ul><li>Another thing to potentially explain the economic prosperity of Asian countries.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Nations typically Westernize, then modernize, then reject Westernization\">Nations typically Westernize, then modernize, then reject Westernization</a><ul><li>The pattern in play here.</li></ul></li></ul>"});
